<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Samarkand</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 750 CE (approx.) · capital Samarkand</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Sogdian city-state, ruled by ikhshids whose merchants ran Silk Road trade networks from Byzantium to China.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, ancient Maracanda, had been the chief city of Sogdiana since Achaemenid times. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire</a> it emerged in the 6th century as the foremost of the Sogdian principalities under rulers with the title ikhshid, recognising the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ikhshids presided over a merchant aristocracy rather than an absolute monarchy. Sogdian traders dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan commerce</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, with communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. The famous murals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>, painted for the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varkhuman">Varkhuman</a> around 660, show embassies from China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and the Turks. Religion was a local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temples</a> and ossuary burial, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in 712, and the ikhshid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghurak">Ghurak</a> ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> while also courting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turgesh-khaganate">Turgesh Khaganate</a>. After the Abbasid takeover around 750 the ikhshids lost power, and the city passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> in the 9th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 500 CE – 750 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:69.65%;width:4.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 750 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Samarkand</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Varkhuman, Ghurak, Tarkhun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sogdian Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sogdian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ikhshidate of Sogdia, Kang, Sogdiana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=625&amp;focus=principality-of-samarkand" aria-label="Principality of Samarkand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6332 758 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Samarkand lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6652 1014L6820 913M6652 1014L7771 1192M66